    alt    WITH c: Y = 2.4846e+08 (> 1/4, past the branch point) -> sin(arg K) = 3.1720e-05
    alt    NO  c: Y = 2.7645e-09 (< 1/4, INSIDE the cut) -> K pure imaginary, arg K = 90.0 deg, sin(arg K) = 1.0000
    => the c-factors are worth ~3.8e+04x in the tangential coefficient, and they are
       the difference between "no galactic problem" and "total galactic destruction".  The map is
       LOAD-BEARING FOR THE RESCUE, which is why it is pinned three independent ways above.
       Frequency inventory vs the branch point a0/2c (canon) -- which physical orbits land inside the cut:
      UGC05721 innermost (binding)     Omega = 5.941e-15  Y = 3.6252e+08  PAST the cut   period = 3.351e-02 Gyr
      Milky Way at the Sun             Omega = 9.208e-16  Y = 8.7082e+06  PAST the cut   period = 2.162e-01 Gyr
      wide binary, 20 kAU              Omega = 7.039e-14  Y = 5.0886e+10  PAST the cut   period = 2.828e-03 Gyr
      Saturn                           Omega = 6.759e-09  Y = 4.6916e+20  PAST the cut   period = 2.946e-08 Gyr
      cluster orbit ~ 1e-16            Omega = 1.000e-16  Y = 1.0270e+05  PAST the cut   period = 1.991e+00 Gyr
      H_Lambda ~ 1.8e-18               Omega = 1.800e-18  Y = 3.3274e+01  PAST the cut   period = 1.106e+02 Gyr
      branch point a0/2c itself        Omega = 1.560e-19  Y = 2.5000e-01  INSIDE the cut   period = 1.276e+03 Gyr

  >>> FINDING 5 [PASS] the dimensional correction z = -(omega c/a0)^2 is correct and triple-pinned.
      Dimensional analysis, the paper's own forced corner a0/2c = 1.5602e-19 rad/s (= the z = -1/4 branch point, quoted in Sec. 5.3 as 1.56e-19 with tau_mem = 2c/a0 = 203/168 Gyr), and the new e-fold floor of Section 4 all give the same c-factors. Moving them (the lead's c-less reading) puts every orbit INSIDE the cut and inflates sin(arg K) to 1.0 -- so the correction is load-bearing for the rescue and is independently anchored, not chosen.

